/* ---------- LISTENING: Academic Talk (4 Q, ~200–250 words) ---------- */
POOL.ltalk = [
 {id:"tk1", v:"buttery", setting:"Biology lecture", title:"Navigation in Migratory Birds",
  s:"Today I want to look at how migratory birds find their way across thousands of kilometers, often on their first journey, without any guidance from adults. For a long time this was a genuine mystery. What we now know is that birds don't rely on a single system, they combine several. First, celestial cues. Many species navigate by the sun during the day and by the stars at night, and planetarium experiments in the sixties showed that young birds actually learn the rotation of the night sky around the pole star. Birds raised under a sky that rotated around a different star simply oriented themselves to that star. Second, and this is the part that still surprises people, birds can detect the Earth's magnetic field. The leading hypothesis is that light-sensitive proteins in the retina react to magnetism, which would mean a bird might literally see the field as a pattern overlaid on its vision. Third, familiar landmarks like coastlines become important near the end of the route. Now, the key concept here is redundancy. If clouds block the stars, the magnetic compass still works. If there's a magnetic storm, the sun is still there. No single cue is reliable all the time, but together they are remarkably robust.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the lecture mainly about?", o:["Why birds migrate in autumn","How birds combine several cues to navigate","The structure of the bird retina","Experiments on young birds in captivity"], a:1, ex:"Hauptthema: Kombination mehrerer Navigationssysteme."},
   {q:"What did the planetarium experiments demonstrate?", o:["Birds are born knowing the position of the pole star","Birds learn to orient themselves to the center of the sky's rotation","Birds cannot see stars indoors","Birds prefer to navigate by the sun"], a:1, ex:"Vögel unter einem Himmel, der sich um einen anderen Stern drehte, orientierten sich an diesem Stern. Sie lernen also das Rotationszentrum."},
   {q:"Why does the professor say birds \"might literally see the field\"?", o:["To describe a hypothesis about how magnetic sensing works","To criticize an outdated theory","To explain why birds avoid storms","To compare birds with insects"], a:0, ex:"Sie beschreibt die führende Hypothese (lichtempfindliche Proteine in der Netzhaut)."},
   {q:"What point does the professor make about redundancy?", o:["Birds waste energy by using several systems","The systems compensate for one another when one fails","Landmarks are the most important cue","Magnetic storms make navigation impossible"], a:1, ex:"Fällt ein Hinweisreiz aus, springt ein anderer ein."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk2", v:"glassy", setting:"Economics lecture", title:"Network Effects and Multi-Homing",
  s:"Let's turn to network effects. A product exhibits network effects when its value to each user increases as more people use it. The textbook example is the telephone. A single phone is worthless, two phones allow exactly one connection, but a million phones create an enormous number of possible connections. Digital platforms work similarly, but often with two sides. On a marketplace, sellers go where the buyers are, and buyers go where the sellers are. This feedback loop tends to produce winner-takes-most outcomes, which is why a handful of platforms dominate many markets. Now, students often conclude from this that dominant platforms are unassailable. That's too quick. What matters is how costly it is for users to participate in several platforms at once, what economists call multi-homing. Ride-hailing is a good case. Drivers and passengers frequently have two or three apps installed and simply compare prices. In that situation, network effects are weak, and a new entrant can compete. Contrast that with professional networks, where your entire history and contacts are locked into one site. So when you evaluate a platform's market power, don't just count users. Ask what it would take for those users to leave.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the main purpose of the talk?", o:["To argue that dominant platforms cannot be challenged","To explain network effects and what limits their power","To explain why the telephone succeeded as a product","To show that ride-hailing markets have strong network effects"], a:1, ex:"Netzwerkeffekte und was ihre Macht begrenzt (Multi-Homing)."},
   {q:"Why does the professor mention ride-hailing apps?", o:["As an example where network effects are relatively weak","To show that one company always dominates","To illustrate a one-sided market","To explain how prices are set"], a:0, ex:"Viele nutzen mehrere Apps gleichzeitig, also schwache Netzwerkeffekte."},
   {q:"What is the professor's attitude toward the conclusion that dominant platforms are \"unassailable\"?", o:["She considers it well supported","She thinks it is premature","She thinks it applies only to telephones","She is uncertain what it means"], a:1, ex:"„That's too quick.“"},
   {q:"Why are professional networks contrasted with ride-hailing?", o:["Users find it harder to switch because their data are locked in","They have fewer users","They are cheaper to use","They do not have network effects"], a:0, ex:"Historie und Kontakte sind auf einer Seite gebunden, also hohe Wechselkosten."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk3", v:"rounded", setting:"Art history lecture", title:"Impressionism and the Paint Tube",
  s:"When we discuss Impressionism, we tend to focus on the artists themselves. But technology played a role that's easy to overlook. Before the 1840s, painters typically ground their own pigments and mixed them with oil, and the result dried quickly once exposed to air. Storing paint in pig bladders was common, and they burst easily. So painting outdoors was impractical, and most finished landscapes were produced in the studio from sketches. Then, in 1841, an American portrait painter living in London patented the collapsible tin tube. Suddenly, artists could carry a full range of ready-mixed colors into the field. This made it feasible to complete a painting on site and to capture fleeting effects of light, which is exactly what the Impressionists became known for. Renoir reportedly remarked that without tubes there would have been no Impressionism. Now, I'd treat that with some caution. The movement also depended on new attitudes toward modern life, on the rejection of academic conventions, and on new exhibition practices. But the anecdote is useful because it reminds us that artistic change often has material preconditions.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the lecture mainly about?", o:["The biography of Renoir","The influence of a technical invention on an artistic movement","How pigments are manufactured","Why studios declined in popularity"], a:1, ex:"Einfluss der Farbtube auf den Impressionismus."},
   {q:"According to the professor, why was outdoor painting difficult before the 1840s?", o:["Landscape painting was not respected","Paint was hard to store and transport and dried quickly","Artists lacked training in landscape","Tin was too expensive"], a:1, ex:"Farbe trocknete schnell, Schweineblasen platzten leicht."},
   {q:"What is the professor's attitude toward Renoir's remark?", o:["She accepts it completely","She considers it an overstatement","She doubts that Renoir said anything about tubes","She thinks it applies only to portraits"], a:1, ex:"„I'd treat that with some caution“: Sie hält es für übertrieben."},
   {q:"What does the professor mean by \"material preconditions\"?", o:["The prices artists could charge","Physical tools and technologies that make new styles possible","The subjects that artists choose to paint","The rules of academic institutions"], a:1, ex:"Materielle Voraussetzungen = Werkzeuge und Technik, die neue Stile ermöglichen."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk4", v:"airy", setting:"Psychology lecture", title:"The Spacing Effect",
  s:"Let's talk about one of the most robust findings in learning research, the spacing effect. If you study material in several shorter sessions spread over time, you'll remember it far better than if you spend the same total time in a single block. This was first documented by Hermann Ebbinghaus in the 1880s, who tested himself on lists of nonsense syllables. Now, here's the puzzle. Students overwhelmingly prefer cramming, and when you ask them afterwards, they actually believe cramming worked better. Why? Because massed practice produces a feeling of fluency. The material seems familiar after the fourth repetition in a row, and that familiarity is mistaken for learning. With spacing, by contrast, you've partly forgotten the material by the next session, so retrieval feels effortful. But that effort is precisely what strengthens the memory. Researchers sometimes call these desirable difficulties. So the practical implication is somewhat counterintuitive. If studying feels easy, that's not necessarily a good sign. A rough guideline is to space reviews further apart as the exam gets further away. For a test in a week, reviewing every day or two works well. For an exam in a year, monthly reviews might be optimal.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the main topic of the lecture?", o:["The life of Hermann Ebbinghaus","Why distributing study sessions over time improves memory","How to memorize nonsense syllables","The disadvantages of studying in groups"], a:1, ex:"Spacing-Effekt."},
   {q:"According to the professor, why do students believe cramming works better?", o:["They receive higher grades after cramming","Repeated exposure creates a misleading sense of familiarity","Professors recommend it","They have more time before exams"], a:1, ex:"Flüssigkeitsgefühl wird mit Lernen verwechselt."},
   {q:"What does the professor mean by \"desirable difficulties\"?", o:["Tasks that are too hard to complete","Effortful retrieval that improves long-term retention","Exams with difficult questions","Distractions during study sessions"], a:1, ex:"Anstrengendes Abrufen stärkt das Gedächtnis."},
   {q:"What can be inferred about the ideal review schedule for a test in three months?", o:["Reviews should happen every hour","Reviews should be spaced further apart than for a test in a week","Only one review is necessary","Reviews should happen only on the day before"], a:1, ex:"Faustregel: Je weiter die Prüfung entfernt, desto größer die Abstände."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk5", v:"buttery", setting:"Environmental science lecture", title:"Urban Trees and Cooling",
  s:"Last week we looked at urban heat islands. Today I want to examine one of the most commonly proposed solutions, planting trees, and why the effect is more complicated than it first appears. Trees cool cities in two main ways. They provide shade, which keeps surfaces like asphalt from absorbing sunlight, and they release water vapor through their leaves, a process called evapotranspiration, which cools the surrounding air. Measurements show that a well-shaded street can be several degrees cooler at ground level. However, and this is often missed, the benefit depends heavily on the type of tree and on water availability. During a drought, trees close the pores in their leaves to conserve water, and evapotranspiration drops sharply, precisely when cooling is needed most. Dense canopies in narrow streets can also trap heat at night by blocking radiation from escaping upward. So cities are increasingly choosing drought-tolerant species and pairing tree planting with water management, for instance by directing rainwater into planting beds rather than storm drains. The broader lesson is that nature-based solutions need to be designed for the specific site, not simply copied from one city to another.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the main purpose of the lecture?", o:["To argue against planting trees in cities","To explain how trees cool cities and the conditions that limit this effect","To describe the history of urban planning","To compare heat islands in different countries"], a:1, ex:"Wie Bäume kühlen und welche Bedingungen den Effekt begrenzen."},
   {q:"According to the professor, why can trees provide less cooling during droughts?", o:["They lose their leaves completely","They reduce water release to conserve moisture","They absorb more sunlight","They grow more slowly"], a:1, ex:"Bäume schließen ihre Spaltöffnungen, also weniger Verdunstung."},
   {q:"Why does the professor mention narrow streets with dense canopies?", o:["To give an example of ideal tree placement","To show a situation in which trees can increase nighttime heat","To explain why trees grow better in narrow streets","To describe evapotranspiration"], a:1, ex:"Dichte Kronen halten nachts die Wärme fest."},
   {q:"What does the professor imply about nature-based solutions?", o:["They work the same way everywhere","They must be adapted to local conditions","They are more expensive than technical solutions","They should replace water management"], a:1, ex:"„designed for the specific site, not simply copied“."}
  ]},
 {id:"tk6", v:"glassy", setting:"Business lecture", title:"First-Mover Advantage",
  s:"There's a widespread belief in business that being first to market is a decisive advantage. The logic seems sound. The first mover can build brand recognition, lock in customers, and secure scarce resources like the best suppliers or locations. And there are cases where this holds. But when researchers looked systematically at market histories, the picture was much less flattering. A well-known study found that a large share of supposed market pioneers had actually failed, and that many of today's market leaders entered years after the pioneer. Think of search engines. The dominant company was nowhere near the first. So why do later entrants often win? First, pioneers bear the cost of educating customers about an entirely new product category. Second, they have to guess what customers want, while followers can observe the pioneer's mistakes and avoid them. Third, technology often shifts during the early years, so the pioneer's initial investments can become obsolete. That doesn't mean timing is irrelevant. First-mover advantages tend to be real when there are strong network effects or high switching costs. Otherwise, being a fast, well-informed follower is frequently the better strategy.",
  qs:[
   {q:"What is the main idea of the talk?", o:["Being first to market almost always guarantees success","The advantages of entering first are often overestimated","Search engines are the most competitive market","Companies should avoid new product categories"], a:1, ex:"First-Mover-Vorteil wird häufig überschätzt."},
   {q:"Why does the professor mention search engines?", o:["To give an example of a successful pioneer","To illustrate that a later entrant became the leader","To explain network effects","To describe customer education"], a:1, ex:"Der heutige Marktführer war nicht der Erste."},
   {q:"Which of the following is NOT given as a reason why followers often succeed?", o:["Pioneers must educate customers","Followers can learn from pioneers' mistakes","Followers usually have more capital","Technology changes can make early investments outdated"], a:2, ex:"Mehr Kapital wird nicht genannt (NOT-Frage)."},
   {q:"Under which condition does the professor say first-mover advantages are most likely to be real?", o:["When products are inexpensive","When network effects or switching costs are strong","When technology changes rapidly","When customers are well informed"], a:1, ex:"„when there are strong network effects or high switching costs“."}
  ]}
];
